==English==
{{was wotd|2009|August|14}}

===Etymology===
{{compound|en|eaves|drop}}, i.e. listening at the eaves of a building.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/ˈiːvzˌdɹɒp/}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|en|/ˈivzˌdɹɑp/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-eavesdrop.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{audio|en|en-au-eavesdrop.ogg|Audio (AU)}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|++}}

# {{senseid|en|to hear a conversation one is not intended to hear}} {{lb|en|ambitransitive}} To [[hear]] a [[conversation]] one is not intended to hear; to [[listen in]].
#: ''I hope nobody was '''eavesdropping''' on our conversation last night, since it got so personal.''
# {{lb|en|zoology}} To listen for another [[organism]]'s [[call]]s, so as to [[exploit]] them.
#* '''1993''', David W. Nagorsen, ‎Robert Mark Brigham, ‎Royal British Columbia Museum, ''Bats of British Columbia'' (page 109)
#*: {{...}} Eastern Red Bat will '''eavesdrop''' on the sonar calls of other red bats to locate potential insect prey.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2014|author=Ken Yasukawa|title=Animal Behavior: How and Why Animals Do the Things They Do|passage=Frog-biting midges also depend on '''eavesdropping''' for reproduction, and one would predict similarly strong selection to evolve auditory adaptations for hearing and localizing calls of their hosts.}}

====Usage notes====
To ''eavesdrop'' usually implies the listener is purposefully trying to hear the conversation of others. To {{m|en|overhear}} is more often accidental.

====Synonyms====
* {{sense|hear conversation one is not intended to hear}} {{l|en|overhear}}

